= Metacognition =

== Overview ==
'''Metacognition''' concerns knowledge, monitoring, and regulation of one's own cognitive processes.

It includes judging what one knows, noticing confusion, estimating confidence, choosing strategies, allocating study time, checking progress, and changing approach when performance is poor.

== Monitoring and control ==
A common distinction is:

'''Monitoring''' — estimating the current state of cognition or performance.

'''Control''' — changing behavior or strategy based on those estimates.

For example, noticing that a chapter is poorly understood is monitoring; deciding to reread it is control.

== Metacognitive knowledge ==
People also hold beliefs about how cognition works:

* what tasks are difficult;
* which strategies are useful;
* what they personally know or forget;
* when external aids are helpful.

These beliefs can be accurate or mistaken.

== Confidence and calibration ==
Confidence is one form of metacognitive judgment.

Good calibration means confidence tracks actual accuracy.

High confidence alone is not evidence of good metacognition.

== Learning ==
Metacognition supports [[Learning]] by helping learners decide what to study, when to test themselves, when to seek help, and whether an answer needs revision.

However, subjective fluency can mislead monitoring. Material that feels easy can still be poorly learned.

== Metacognition and introspection ==
Metacognition is broader than introspection.

It includes prospective judgments, performance monitoring, strategic control, and behavioral regulation, not only conscious observation of inner experience.

== Related concepts ==
[[Uncertainty]], [[Epistemic status]], and [[Reasoning]] connect monitoring/control to confidence, self-evaluation, and the regulation of reasoning.

